window.NABLA_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Nabla Lab | Independent Academic Community","url":"/","description":"INDEPENDENT ACADEMIC COMMUNITY Shared beginnings. Connected through research. A community with roots at HIT Weihai, continuing collaborative research across institutions and sharin","searchText":"INDEPENDENT ACADEMIC COMMUNITY Shared beginnings. Connected through research. A community with roots at HIT Weihai, continuing collaborative research across institutions and sharing research experience. Explore our research → Fluids · Marine engineering · Aerodynamics NABLA LAB · EST. 2025 ABOUT US A shared beginning, many academic paths. Nabla Lab is an independent academic community founded in 2025 by a student-led group at Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai. Our members share a joint undergraduate background with the University of Strathclyde. As our academic paths lead to different institutions, we keep working together on research questions and shared projects, while supporting research training and exchanging experience. Discover our story ↗ OUR RESEARCH Connected interests. Explore all themes ↗ 01 FLOW & COMPUTATION Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation ↗ Understanding fluid motion through mechanics and computational approaches. Our interests include computational fluid dynamics and physics-informed neural networks as ways to study fluid behaviour. 02 WAVES, STRUCTURES & OCEANS Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering ↗ Exploring how waves, fluids and structures interact in marine settings. This theme brings together interests in wave–structure interaction, fluid–structure interaction, structural mechanics, marine engineering and offshore renewable energy. 03 AERODYNAMICS & ACOUSTICS Aerodynamics &amp; Noise ↗ Studying aerodynamic behaviour and noise associated with unmanned aerial vehicles. Current interests connect fluid mechanics and aerodynamics with the question of UAV noise reduction. 04 LOW-ALTITUDE APPLICATIONS Low-altitude Economy ↗ An area of interest connecting low-altitude aviation with engineering questions. Within this theme, our current interests include the aerodynamics and noise of unmanned aerial vehicles. PEOPLE Meet our community. Explore member profiles ↗ A circle of peers, connected by curiosity. Pause motion ∇ Shared curiosity. Collective discovery. Yongqi Yuan 袁泳棋 Oxford ↗ Jiahe Tian 田嘉禾 Strathclyde ↗ Yu Zhao 赵煜 HIT Weihai · Strathclyde ↗ Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 HIT Weihai · Strathclyde ↗ Zhirui Ju 具志睿 HIT Weihai · Strathclyde ↗ OUR HISTORY From research training to a continuing community. Nabla Lab was established on 1 March 2025. Earlier CFD work laid the groundwork. Our story has grown through wave simulations, research training and the people who joined along the way. Explore our timeline ↗ NABLA EDITORIAL · NABLA 社论 Thinking through a changing world. Recording questions about research, technology and life — and how we change our minds. Explore Nabla Editorial ↗ FOUNDING NOTE · 创刊说明 An Exciting and Unsettling Age 在一个令人兴奋，也令人不安的时代 袁泳棋 / Yongqi Yuan · 18 October 2026 ↗ NEWS Notes from Nabla. All news ↗ NABLA LAB LAB NEWS 9 October 2026 A new home for Nabla Lab ↗ Our community, research interests and academic profiles now have a shared home at nabla.ac.cn. Read the announcement → OCEAN ENGINEERING 353 ARTICLE 124671 · 2026 PUBLICATION 2 March 2026 Gap-resonance study published in Ocean Engineering ↗ A paper by Yongqi Yuan, Jiahe Tian and Yu Zhao examines how corner configurations affect fluid resonance between adjacent boxes. Explore the paper → STAY CONNECTED Academic paths evolve. Connections continue. Explore our members and the research interests that connect Nabla Lab. Meet our people ↗"},{"title":"About us","url":"/about/","description":"Home / About us NABLA LAB About us Shared beginnings. Continuing research together. WHO WE ARE An independent academic community. Nabla Lab began as a student-led group at Harbin I","searchText":"Home / About us NABLA LAB About us Shared beginnings. Continuing research together. WHO WE ARE An independent academic community. Nabla Lab began as a student-led group at Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai in 2025. Our early activities centred on research and research training. Established on 1 March 2025, the group brought a shared interest in CFD and wave–structure interaction into a common space for learning and investigation. Today, our members pursue studies and research at different institutions. Nabla Lab provides a continuing connection through which we work on shared research questions. Explore our history ↗ Established 2025 · Connected through research OUR PURPOSE Continuing research together. 01 / PRIMARY FOCUS Collaborative research Discuss research questions and advance shared projects as our members pursue academic paths at different institutions. 02 / SUPPORTING FOCUS Research training &amp; experience Support one another’s development through research training and the exchange of methods and practical experience. OUR NAME The name Nabla. Nabla Lab takes its name from ∇ (nabla), the differential operator that appears in the Navier–Stokes equations describing fluid motion. The name reflects our shared interest in fluid mechanics. SHARED UNDERGRADUATE BACKGROUND Two institutions, a common beginning. Our members have studied, or are studying, in the joint undergraduate programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Our current academic roles and institutions are listed in each member’s profile. Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai ↗ (opens in a new tab) University of Strathclyde ↗ (opens in a new tab) Meet our people ↗ STAY CONNECTED Academic paths evolve. Connections continue. Explore our members and the research interests that connect Nabla Lab. Meet our people ↗"},{"title":"Our research","url":"/research/","description":"Home / Our research NABLA LAB Our research Research interests across fluid mechanics, computation, marine engineering and low-altitude applications. RESEARCH THEMES Explore our int","searchText":"Home / Our research NABLA LAB Our research Research interests across fluid mechanics, computation, marine engineering and low-altitude applications. RESEARCH THEMES Explore our interests. 01 FLOW & COMPUTATION Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation ↗ Understanding fluid motion through mechanics and computational approaches. Our interests include computational fluid dynamics and physics-informed neural networks as ways to study fluid behaviour. 02 WAVES, STRUCTURES & OCEANS Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering ↗ Exploring how waves, fluids and structures interact in marine settings. This theme brings together interests in wave–structure interaction, fluid–structure interaction, structural mechanics, marine engineering and offshore renewable energy. 03 AERODYNAMICS & ACOUSTICS Aerodynamics &amp; Noise ↗ Studying aerodynamic behaviour and noise associated with unmanned aerial vehicles. Current interests connect fluid mechanics and aerodynamics with the question of UAV noise reduction. 04 LOW-ALTITUDE APPLICATIONS Low-altitude Economy ↗ An area of interest connecting low-altitude aviation with engineering questions. Within this theme, our current interests include the aerodynamics and noise of unmanned aerial vehicles. These themes reflect our members’ research and academic interests. Explore the individual profiles for their backgrounds and focus. PUBLICATIONS Published research. View publications ↗ Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ PEOPLE &amp; RESEARCH Different questions. Shared connections. From CFD and physics-informed neural networks to offshore renewable energy and UAV noise reduction, our interests follow the academic paths of our members. Meet the people behind the themes ↗"},{"title":"Publications","url":"/research/publications/","description":"Home / Our research / Publications RESEARCH OUTPUTS Publications Published research by members of Nabla Lab. 2026 Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap ","searchText":"Home / Our research / Publications RESEARCH OUTPUTS Publications Published research by members of Nabla Lab. 2026 Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ Exploring gap resonance. Using a two-dimensional viscous-flow model in OpenFOAM, the study examines fluid resonance between two fixed boxes with sharp, chamfered or rounded corners. It explores how corner shape, corner dimensions and incoming wave amplitude affect resonance and nonlinear wave responses. Smoothing the corners shifts resonance towards higher frequencies and increases the resonant wave response. The study connects these changes to flow separation, viscous dissipation and higher-harmonic contributions. Waves, structures and marine engineering ↗"},{"title":"Fluid Mechanics & Computation","url":"/research/fluid-mechanics/","description":"Home / Our research / Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation FLOW & COMPUTATION Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation Understanding fluid motion through mechanics and computational approach","searchText":"Home / Our research / Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation FLOW & COMPUTATION Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation Understanding fluid motion through mechanics and computational approaches. Our interests include computational fluid dynamics and physics-informed neural networks as ways to study fluid behaviour. Conceptual illustration AREAS OF INTEREST Questions that connect us. Understanding fluid motion through mechanics and computational approaches. Our interests include computational fluid dynamics and physics-informed neural networks as ways to study fluid behaviour. Computational fluid dynamics (CFD) Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) PEOPLE &amp; THIS THEME Explore the academic profiles. Yongqi Yuan 袁泳棋 DPhil student in Engineering Science ↗ Jiahe Tian 田嘉禾 PhD student ↗ Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student ↗ Back to all research themes ←"},{"title":"Waves, Structures & Marine Engineering","url":"/research/wave-loading/","description":"Home / Our research / Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering WAVES, STRUCTURES & OCEANS Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering Exploring how waves, fluids and structures in","searchText":"Home / Our research / Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering WAVES, STRUCTURES & OCEANS Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering Exploring how waves, fluids and structures interact in marine settings. This theme brings together interests in wave–structure interaction, fluid–structure interaction, structural mechanics, marine engineering and offshore renewable energy. Conceptual illustration AREAS OF INTEREST Questions that connect us. Exploring how waves, fluids and structures interact in marine settings. This theme brings together interests in wave–structure interaction, fluid–structure interaction, structural mechanics, marine engineering and offshore renewable energy. Fluid–structure interaction &amp; structural mechanics Offshore renewable energy PEOPLE &amp; THIS THEME Explore the academic profiles. Yongqi Yuan 袁泳棋 DPhil student in Engineering Science ↗ Jiahe Tian 田嘉禾 PhD student ↗ Yu Zhao 赵煜 Undergraduate student ↗ Zhirui Ju 具志睿 Undergraduate student ↗ Back to all research themes ←"},{"title":"Aerodynamics & Noise","url":"/research/aerodynamics-noise/","description":"Home / Our research / Aerodynamics &amp; Noise AERODYNAMICS & ACOUSTICS Aerodynamics &amp; Noise Studying aerodynamic behaviour and noise associated with unmanned aerial vehicles. ","searchText":"Home / Our research / Aerodynamics &amp; Noise AERODYNAMICS & ACOUSTICS Aerodynamics &amp; Noise Studying aerodynamic behaviour and noise associated with unmanned aerial vehicles. Current interests connect fluid mechanics and aerodynamics with the question of UAV noise reduction. Conceptual illustration AREAS OF INTEREST Questions that connect us. Studying aerodynamic behaviour and noise associated with unmanned aerial vehicles. Current interests connect fluid mechanics and aerodynamics with the question of UAV noise reduction. Aerodynamics UAV noise reduction PEOPLE &amp; THIS THEME Explore the academic profiles. Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student ↗ Back to all research themes ←"},{"title":"Low-altitude Economy","url":"/research/low-altitude-economy/","description":"Home / Our research / Low-altitude Economy LOW-ALTITUDE APPLICATIONS Low-altitude Economy An area of interest connecting low-altitude aviation with engineering questions. Within th","searchText":"Home / Our research / Low-altitude Economy LOW-ALTITUDE APPLICATIONS Low-altitude Economy An area of interest connecting low-altitude aviation with engineering questions. Within this theme, our current interests include the aerodynamics and noise of unmanned aerial vehicles. Conceptual illustration AREAS OF INTEREST Questions that connect us. An area of interest connecting low-altitude aviation with engineering questions. Within this theme, our current interests include the aerodynamics and noise of unmanned aerial vehicles. Low-altitude economy UAV aerodynamics &amp; noise PEOPLE &amp; THIS THEME Explore the academic profiles. Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student ↗ Back to all research themes ←"},{"title":"Research practice","url":"/research/research-practice/","description":"Home / Research practice NABLA LAB Research practice Clear questions, assumptions and communication. Conceptual illustration Learning through research. Research and research traini","searchText":"Home / Research practice NABLA LAB Research practice Clear questions, assumptions and communication. Conceptual illustration Learning through research. Research and research training have been part of Nabla Lab since its beginnings. We want to share the questions, assumptions and methods that shape our work, alongside our developing research interests. Explore our research history ↗"},{"title":"People","url":"/people/","description":"Home / People NABLA LAB People Researchers and students with shared beginnings and different academic paths. OUR COMMUNITY Meet our members. Our members have studied, or are studyi","searchText":"Home / People NABLA LAB People Researchers and students with shared beginnings and different academic paths. OUR COMMUNITY Meet our members. Our members have studied, or are studying, in the joint undergraduate programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Current roles and affiliations are listed below. Our shared background ↗ ACADEMIC PROFILE Yongqi Yuan ↗ 袁泳棋 DPhil student in Engineering Science University of Oxford Lady Margaret Hall Wave–structure interaction Fluid mechanics Computational fluid dynamics (CFD) View academic profile → JT ACADEMIC PROFILE Jiahe Tian ↗ 田嘉禾 PhD student University of Strathclyde Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory Offshore renewable energy Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) Wave–structure interaction View academic profile → YZ ACADEMIC PROFILE Yu Zhao ↗ 赵煜 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Fluid–structure interaction Structural mechanics Wave–structure interaction View academic profile → ZH ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhuoxi Hu ↗ 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde UAV noise reduction Low-altitude economy Fluid mechanics Aerodynamics View academic profile → ZJ ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhirui Ju ↗ 具志睿 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Marine engineering View academic profile →"},{"title":"Our history","url":"/about/history/","description":"Home / About us / Our history ESTABLISHED 2025 Our history Research, research training and the people who brought Nabla Lab together. Nabla Lab was established on 1 March 2025. Our","searchText":"Home / About us / Our history ESTABLISHED 2025 Our history Research, research training and the people who brought Nabla Lab together. Nabla Lab was established on 1 March 2025. Our story began with earlier CFD work, and continues through shared research interests and academic connections. 2023–2024 Preparatory CFD work Wave-generation and validation work in ANSYS Fluent prepared the foundation for later studies. 9 December 2024 OpenFOAM environment An OpenFOAM and Ubuntu environment was established. February 2025 First OpenFOAM case The first waves2Foam/OpenFOAM case was set up on 26 February; the toolbox was loaded on 28 February. 1 March 2025 Nabla Lab established Nabla Lab was established as a student-led group; Yu Zhao was invited to join. 1 April 2025 Gap-resonance study The gap-resonance study began. 4 June 2025 Key technical progress Progress was made on key techniques for the study. 27 June 2025 Zhuoxi Hu invited to join Zhuoxi Hu was invited to join Nabla Lab. 7 August 2025 Main CFD work completed The main CFD work for the gap-resonance study was completed. 14 August–8 November 2025 Analysis, figures and writing Results were revisited, with data analysis, figure preparation and writing continuing through early November. November–December 2025 Writing and revision Manuscript writing and revisions continued, with completion on 14 December. 24 November 2025 Zhirui Ju invited to join Zhirui Ju was invited to join Nabla Lab. 19 December 2025 Submitted to Ocean Engineering A manuscript was submitted to Ocean Engineering. 2 March 2026 Published in Ocean Engineering The gap-resonance paper by Yongqi Yuan, Jiahe Tian and Yu Zhao was published online as the version of record in Ocean Engineering. Explore the publication ↗ 9 October 2026 New website The Nabla Lab website brings the community together at nabla.ac.cn. Meet the people in our story ↗"},{"title":"Jiahe Tian","url":"/people/jiahe-tian/","description":"Home / People / Jiahe Tian PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Jiahe Tian 田嘉禾 PhD student University of Strathclyde Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory Offshore renewable energy Explore research","searchText":"Home / People / Jiahe Tian PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Jiahe Tian 田嘉禾 PhD student University of Strathclyde Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory Offshore renewable energy Explore research interests → JT NABLA LAB · ACADEMIC COMMUNITY About Research Publications Education Links 01 / ABOUT An academic introduction. Jiahe is a PhD student in Strathclyde’s Department of Naval Architecture, Ocean &amp; Marine Engineering and Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory. Research interests include offshore renewable energy, physics-informed neural networks and wave–structure interaction. 02 / RESEARCH Research interests. Offshore renewable energy Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) Wave–structure interaction Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation ↗ Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering ↗ 03 / PUBLICATIONS Published research. Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ All publications at Nabla Lab ↗ 04 / EDUCATION A shared undergraduate background. Shared undergraduate background: the joint programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. About our shared background ↗ 05 / ACADEMIC LINKS Academic connections. UNDERGRADUATE BACKGROUND Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai ↗ Joint undergraduate programme (opens in a new tab) DEPARTMENT Naval Architecture, Ocean and Marine Engineering ↗ University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) LABORATORY Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory ↗ University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) © 2026 Jiahe Tian Back to People ← ACADEMIC DETAILS At a glance. Position PhD student Institution University of Strathclyde Department Department of Naval Architecture, Ocean &amp; Marine Engineering Laboratory Kelvin Hydrodynamics Laboratory People at Nabla Lab ↗"},{"title":"Yu Zhao","url":"/people/yu-zhao/","description":"Home / People / Yu Zhao PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Yu Zhao 赵煜 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Fluid–structure interaction Explore research interests → Y","searchText":"Home / People / Yu Zhao PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Yu Zhao 赵煜 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Fluid–structure interaction Explore research interests → YZ NABLA LAB · ACADEMIC COMMUNITY About Research Publications Education Links 01 / ABOUT An academic introduction. Yu is an undergraduate in the joint undergraduate programme between HIT Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Research interests include fluid–structure interaction, structural mechanics and wave–structure interaction. 02 / RESEARCH Research interests. Fluid–structure interaction Structural mechanics Wave–structure interaction Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering ↗ 03 / PUBLICATIONS Published research. Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ All publications at Nabla Lab ↗ 04 / EDUCATION A shared undergraduate background. Shared undergraduate background: the joint programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. About our shared background ↗ 05 / ACADEMIC LINKS Academic connections. UNDERGRADUATE BACKGROUND Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai ↗ Joint undergraduate programme (opens in a new tab) DEPARTMENT Naval Architecture, Ocean and Marine Engineering ↗ University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) © 2026 Yu Zhao Back to People ← ACADEMIC DETAILS At a glance. Position Undergraduate student Institution HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde People at Nabla Lab ↗"},{"title":"Zhuoxi Hu","url":"/people/zhuoxi-hu/","description":"Home / People / Zhuoxi Hu PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde UAV noise reduction Explore research interests → ZH N","searchText":"Home / People / Zhuoxi Hu PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhuoxi Hu 胡卓曦 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde UAV noise reduction Explore research interests → ZH NABLA LAB · ACADEMIC COMMUNITY About Research Education Academic links 01 / ABOUT An academic introduction. Zhuoxi is an undergraduate in the joint undergraduate programme between HIT Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Research interests include UAV noise reduction, the low-altitude economy, fluid mechanics and aerodynamics. 02 / RESEARCH Research interests. UAV noise reduction Low-altitude economy Fluid mechanics Aerodynamics Fluid Mechanics &amp; Computation ↗ Aerodynamics &amp; Noise ↗ Low-altitude Economy ↗ 03 / EDUCATION A shared undergraduate background. Shared undergraduate background: the joint programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. About our shared background ↗ 04 / ACADEMIC LINKS Academic connections. UNDERGRADUATE BACKGROUND Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai ↗ Joint undergraduate programme (opens in a new tab) DEPARTMENT Naval Architecture, Ocean and Marine Engineering ↗ University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) © 2026 Zhuoxi Hu Back to People ← ACADEMIC DETAILS At a glance. Position Undergraduate student Institution HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde People at Nabla Lab ↗"},{"title":"Zhirui Ju","url":"/people/zhirui-ju/","description":"Home / People / Zhirui Ju PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhirui Ju 具志睿 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Marine engineering Explore research interests → ZJ NA","searchText":"Home / People / Zhirui Ju PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Zhirui Ju 具志睿 Undergraduate student HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde Marine engineering Explore research interests → ZJ NABLA LAB · ACADEMIC COMMUNITY About Research Education Academic links 01 / ABOUT An academic introduction. Zhirui is an undergraduate in the joint undergraduate programme between HIT Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Research interests include marine engineering. Further research details will be added as they become available. 02 / RESEARCH Research interests. Marine engineering Further research details will be added as they become available. Waves, Structures &amp; Marine Engineering ↗ 03 / EDUCATION A shared undergraduate background. Shared undergraduate background: the joint programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. About our shared background ↗ 04 / ACADEMIC LINKS Academic connections. UNDERGRADUATE BACKGROUND Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai ↗ Joint undergraduate programme (opens in a new tab) DEPARTMENT Naval Architecture, Ocean and Marine Engineering ↗ University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) © 2026 Zhirui Ju Back to People ← ACADEMIC DETAILS At a glance. Position Undergraduate student Institution HIT Weihai · University of Strathclyde People at Nabla Lab ↗"},{"title":"Yongqi Yuan","url":"/people/yongqi-yuan/","description":"Home / People / Yongqi Yuan PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Yongqi Yuan 袁泳棋 DPhil student in Engineering Science University of Oxford · Lady Margaret Hall Studying wave loading at Oxford","searchText":"Home / People / Yongqi Yuan PERSONAL ACADEMIC PROFILE Yongqi Yuan 袁泳棋 DPhil student in Engineering Science University of Oxford · Lady Margaret Hall Studying wave loading at Oxford. Explore my research → About Education Research Publications Links 01 / ABOUT An academic introduction. I’m Yongqi Yuan ( 袁泳棋 ), a DPhil student in Engineering Science at the University of Oxford. My current academic status is Probationary Research Student (PRS). I am a member of Thomas Adcock’s research group (opens in a new tab) and affiliated with Lady Margaret Hall. My research interests include fluid mechanics, wave–structure interaction and computational fluid dynamics. Education and academic background ↗ 02 / EDUCATION Education. DPhil in Engineering Science University of Oxford (opens in a new tab) October 2026 – present Oxford, UK Probationary Research Student (PRS) BEng (Hons) in Naval Architecture with Ocean Engineering University of Strathclyde (opens in a new tab) September 2022 – June 2026 Glasgow, UK First Class Honours BEng in Naval Architecture and Marine Engineering Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai (opens in a new tab) August 2022 – June 2026 Weihai, China JOINT UNDERGRADUATE PROGRAMME My undergraduate background is in the joint programme between Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai and the University of Strathclyde. Our shared background ↗ 03 / RESEARCH Fluids, waves &amp; computation. My research interests centre on fluid mechanics, wave–structure interaction and computational fluid dynamics (CFD). At Oxford, my work concerns wave loading within a research group that studies ocean hydrodynamics and the interaction between waves and infrastructure. Explore fluid mechanics and computation ↗ Conceptual illustration of wave–structure interaction WAVES &amp; STRUCTURES From fluid motion to physical forces. Explore waves and marine engineering ↗ About the research group ↗ (opens in a new tab) 04 / PUBLICATIONS Publications &amp; academic writing. Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ All publications at Nabla Lab ↗ 05 / ACADEMIC LINKS Profiles &amp; academic connections. ORCID 0009-0004-7632-3743 ↗ Researcher identifier and academic record (opens in a new tab) GITHUB yongqi-yuan ↗ Personal GitHub profile (opens in a new tab) RESEARCH GROUP Thomas Adcock ↗ Department of Engineering Science, Oxford (opens in a new tab) COLLEGE Lady Margaret Hall ↗ University of Oxford (opens in a new tab) INSTITUTION University of Oxford ↗ University website (opens in a new tab) © 2026 Yongqi Yuan Back to People ← ACADEMIC DETAILS At a glance. Position DPhil student Degree programme DPhil in Engineering Science Current status Probationary Research Student (PRS) Institution University of Oxford College Lady Margaret Hall Research interests Fluid mechanics Wave–structure interaction Computational fluid dynamics Research group Thomas Adcock ↗ (opens in a new tab) People at Nabla Lab ↗"},{"title":"News","url":"/news/","description":"Home / News NABLA LAB News Research notes, lab updates and the stories behind our work. LATEST UPDATE From the lab. NABLA LAB LAB NEWS 9 October 2026 A new home for Nabla Lab ↗ Our","searchText":"Home / News NABLA LAB News Research notes, lab updates and the stories behind our work. LATEST UPDATE From the lab. NABLA LAB LAB NEWS 9 October 2026 A new home for Nabla Lab ↗ Our community, research interests and academic profiles now have a shared home at nabla.ac.cn. Read the announcement → OCEAN ENGINEERING 353 ARTICLE 124671 · 2026 PUBLICATION 2 March 2026 Gap-resonance study published in Ocean Engineering ↗ A paper by Yongqi Yuan, Jiahe Tian and Yu Zhao examines how corner configurations affect fluid resonance between adjacent boxes. Explore the paper →"},{"title":"A new home for Nabla Lab","url":"/news/a-new-home/","description":"Home / News / A new home LAB NEWS A new home for Nabla Lab Our research interests and academic profiles now have a shared home at nabla.ac.cn. 9 October 2026 Welcome to the new Nab","searchText":"Home / News / A new home LAB NEWS A new home for Nabla Lab Our research interests and academic profiles now have a shared home at nabla.ac.cn. 9 October 2026 Welcome to the new Nabla Lab website. The site brings together our research themes, people and future news in one place. Nabla Lab is an independent academic community with shared beginnings at Harbin Institute of Technology, Weihai. Our members’ interests connect fluid mechanics, computation, marine engineering, aerodynamics and low-altitude applications. Visitors can explore the questions behind our research, find individual academic backgrounds and return for updates as new work becomes available. The visual identity uses the original Nabla Laboratory logos, with their deep blue and red palette. Explore the research themes ↗"},{"title":"Gap-resonance study published in Ocean Engineering","url":"/news/gap-resonance-paper/","description":"Home / News / Gap-resonance publication PUBLICATION Gap-resonance study published in Ocean Engineering Research by Yongqi Yuan, Jiahe Tian and Yu Zhao. 2 March 2026 Our gap-resonan","searchText":"Home / News / Gap-resonance publication PUBLICATION Gap-resonance study published in Ocean Engineering Research by Yongqi Yuan, Jiahe Tian and Yu Zhao. 2 March 2026 Our gap-resonance study is published in Ocean Engineering . Using a two-dimensional viscous-flow model in OpenFOAM, the study examines fluid resonance between two fixed boxes with sharp, chamfered or rounded corners. It explores how corner shape, corner dimensions and incoming wave amplitude affect resonance and nonlinear wave responses. Smoothing the corners shifts resonance towards higher frequencies and increases the resonant wave response. The study connects these changes to flow separation, viscous dissipation and higher-harmonic contributions. Published 2026 · RESEARCH PAPER Fluid resonance in the narrow gap between side-by-side boxes with different corner configurations (opens in a new tab) Yongqi Yuan , Jiahe Tian , Yu Zhao Ocean Engineering 353, Part 2, Article 124671 (2026) Published online 2 March 2026 Issue date 30 April 2026 DOI: 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.124671 ↗ (opens in a new tab) View at publisher ↗ (opens in a new tab) Download BibTeX ↓ Follow the research history ↗"},{"title":"Contact","url":"/contact/","description":"Home / Contact NABLA LAB Contact Keeping our members connected through research. OUR CURRENT FOCUS Collaboration within our community. At present, Nabla Lab primarily supports coll","searchText":"Home / Contact NABLA LAB Contact Keeping our members connected through research. OUR CURRENT FOCUS Collaboration within our community. At present, Nabla Lab primarily supports collaboration among its existing members. We discuss research questions, share progress and exchange practical experience as our members work on shared projects across institutions. Member profiles bring together our academic backgrounds and research interests. Explore member profiles ↗ Nabla Lab Current focus Member collaboration Website nabla.ac.cn ↗ Academic profiles Meet our members ↗"},{"title":"Copyright & credits","url":"/copyright/","description":"Home / Copyright &amp; credits NABLA LAB Copyright &amp; credits The visual identity and materials behind this website. Website copyright © 2026 Nabla Lab. All rights reserved. Nab","searchText":"Home / Copyright &amp; credits NABLA LAB Copyright &amp; credits The visual identity and materials behind this website. Website copyright © 2026 Nabla Lab. All rights reserved. Nabla visual identity This website uses the original Nabla Laboratory logo artwork supplied by Nabla Lab. Its blue, red and black colours and vector outlines are preserved. Logo artwork is included as supplied. Any reuse of the Nabla identity requires permission from its rights holder. View original wordmark PDF ↗ (opens in a new tab) View original symbol PDF ↗ (opens in a new tab) Imagery &amp; illustrations The ocean visual was created for this website using OpenAI ImageGen. It is an illustrative image, rather than a record of a particular location or experiment. Research diagrams are conceptual illustrations. Portrait supplied by Yongqi Yuan. The name Nabla The name and its connection to fluid mechanics were explained by Nabla Lab. The use of ∇ in the Navier–Stokes equations follows the MIT OpenCourseWare course notes ↗ (opens in a new tab) . Team and academic information The team’s history, shared undergraduate background and member interests were provided by Nabla Lab. Institutional links appear in the individual academic profiles. Publication information Paper titles, author order and bibliographic details follow the publisher’s record. The online publication date and issue date are listed separately. Publisher record ↗ (opens in a new tab) Academic profile sources Yongqi Yuan’s academic affiliation and wave-loading research context are based on the University of Oxford’s official research-group page ↗ (opens in a new tab) ."},{"title":"Nabla Editorial","url":"/editorial/","description":"RESEARCH · TECHNOLOGY · LIFE Nabla Editorial Nabla 社论 A record of questions, perspectives and life in a changing world. Emerging from group discussions, this series records young r","searchText":"RESEARCH · TECHNOLOGY · LIFE Nabla Editorial Nabla 社论 A record of questions, perspectives and life in a changing world. Emerging from group discussions, this series records young researchers’ reflections on research, technology, careers and life. It remains open to disagreement and preserves how perspectives change over time. THE ARCHIVE 2026 01 essay FOUNDING NOTE · 创刊说明 An Exciting and Unsettling Age 在一个令人兴奋，也令人不安的时代 Nabla Editorial begins as a record of how young researchers experience a changing world. Emerging from informal group discussions, this series explores research, technology, work and everyday life, with particular attention to the opportunities and uncertainties of artificial intelligence. It aims to preserve questions, disagreements and evolving perspectives, rather than predict the future. 创刊说明 / Founding note 袁泳棋 / Yongqi Yuan 18 October 2026 Read the founding note ↗ About this series A voice for every member All Nabla members may contribute signed articles. Each piece expresses its author’s perspective, without requiring a team consensus. Preserving ideas in their time Original arguments and first publication dates are preserved. Later perspectives appear in new essays or dated annotations and postscripts; corrections remain documented. Respecting everyone in a discussion Members’ views are quoted with consent and, where appropriate, anonymised by agreement. Reader feedback is currently welcomed by email. Continue the conversation Questions, differing views and feedback are welcome by email. Correspondence will not be quoted with attribution without consent. yongqi.yuan@eng.ox.ac.uk ↗"},{"title":"An Exciting and Unsettling Age","url":"/editorial/an-exciting-and-unsettling-age/","description":"Home / Nabla Editorial / Founding note NABLA EDITORIAL · 001 An Exciting and Unsettling Age 在一个令人兴奋，也令人不安的时代 创刊说明 / Founding note 袁泳棋 / Yongqi Yuan 18 October 2026 Abstract Nabla E","searchText":"Home / Nabla Editorial / Founding note NABLA EDITORIAL · 001 An Exciting and Unsettling Age 在一个令人兴奋，也令人不安的时代 创刊说明 / Founding note 袁泳棋 / Yongqi Yuan 18 October 2026 Abstract Nabla Editorial begins as a record of how young researchers experience a changing world. Emerging from informal group discussions, this series explores research, technology, work and everyday life, with particular attention to the opportunities and uncertainties of artificial intelligence. It aims to preserve questions, disagreements and evolving perspectives, rather than predict the future. English translation · 19 October 2026. 中文原文 first published 18 October 2026. This is an age that is both exciting and unsettling. We want to keep a thoughtful record of what it means to live through it. Beginning with Group Meetings Nabla Editorial grew out of our regular group meetings. Alongside specific research questions, I wanted us to discuss research, technology, careers, money, society and personal life. Some thoughts arise from experience; others are simply observations about recent events. They are not always systematic or fully developed. As a doctoral student working in computational fluid dynamics, fluid mechanics and related engineering research, I want to preserve these questions and reflections. They began as informal conversations within our team. Thinking about artificial intelligence and its social consequences, however, has gradually made me feel that such records might matter beyond our meetings. Excitement and Unease This is an age that is both exciting and unsettling. Artificial intelligence is changing how we acquire knowledge, write code, analyse data and carry out scientific computation. New tools can help us complete many once time-consuming tasks more quickly. Research ideas previously constrained by individual skills, tools and resources may gradually become feasible. For young researchers, the range of things we can attempt is expanding. That is exciting. Yet opportunity arrives with uncertainty. Will the knowledge and skills we invest in today retain their value? What abilities are several years of doctoral training really developing? As AI becomes better at reading papers, writing code, running simulations and analysing data, what remains at the heart of a human researcher's contribution? These questions extend beyond research. Can greater efficiency lead to better lives? Can the benefits of technology be shared more widely? How will employment, education and research assessment change? There are no ready answers. What unsettles me is not only the technology itself, but also the pace of change and whether we can understand and adapt to it. What Role Will We Play? We may be living through a new industrial revolution driven by artificial intelligence. That is a possibility worth examining, rather than a certain conclusion about the future. What excites us is the possibility that we are witnessing history; what unsettles us is that we do not know what role we will play in it. We benefit from technology while also bearing its uncertainties. We welcome more efficient research, yet ask whether the meaning of research itself is changing. These tensions need not be resolved immediately. They are part of the experience of our time and deserve to be recorded. Nabla Editorial therefore aims to remain open, rational and honest: attentive to new possibilities and serious about their problems, hopeful about the future and candid about our doubts. Why Keep a Record? People looking back on an era often already know its outcomes: which technologies succeeded, which predictions failed and which choices changed lives. Those living through it do not. An uncertain future is part of the experience of the present. I am interested in how ordinary people, especially young researchers, understand and experience these changes. How does a doctoral student in 2026 see AI? How do they understand the value of research and their career prospects? How do they balance research, income, social relationships and everyday life? Recording these reflections preserves more than conclusions. It also preserves the circumstances, expectations and limitations in which ideas formed. Today's judgments may later prove mistaken, but a thoughtful and honest record can still show how people understood the world at the time. We need not pretend to predict the future, nor be right in every instalment. Sometimes a good unanswered question is worth writing down. Letting Ideas Carry the Marks of Time Nabla Editorial will preserve articles' first publication dates and original arguments. Later understanding can be added through new articles, dated postscripts or annotations. Factual or textual corrections should identify what changed and when, so that later knowledge does not silently overwrite earlier judgments. We want to record processes, not just conclusions. Disagreements and questions that produce no consensus also deserve attention. Before quoting another member publicly, we should obtain their consent. Where appropriate, we should anonymise their remarks and confirm that the proposed anonymity respects their wishes. All Nabla members may contribute signed articles. Each author retains their own voice. Articles represent the named author's thinking at the time of publication; they need not express a shared position of the team. Writing for the Future, and for the Present Beginning with research and everyday life, Nabla Editorial will continue to discuss technology, careers, money, society and our relationship with the times we live in. It has no predetermined set of complete answers and promises no fixed publication schedule. We hope to leave a thoughtful record when there is something worth considering. Perhaps, years from now, these writings will offer a small but authentic glimpse of the history of a new industrial revolution. Even if they do not, they will record how we asked questions, exchanged ideas and sought our place amid change. That is why we are beginning to write. Publication record First published: 18 October 2026 . This is the founding note. Dated corrections or additions will be recorded here. © 2026 袁泳棋 / Yongqi Yuan. This article represents the named author’s views. Continue the conversation Questions, differing views and feedback are welcome by email. Correspondence will not be quoted with attribution without consent. yongqi.yuan@eng.ox.ac.uk ↗ Back to Nabla Editorial ↗"}];
